tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Gowdy: \"Superorganisms, Crazy Ants, and Fire Apes, Oh My!\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts ecological economist John Gowdy — 'my friend and one of my intellectual mentors,' per Nate's own direct words, with a real, stated ~20-year prior relationship — to trace the evolutionary and economic origins of the human superorganism. Gowdy argues evolutionary biology has moved well beyond the 'selfish gene' model (epigenetics, group/multi-level selection, culturally-contingent cooperation — citing a real Chinese rice-vs-wheat farming study where rice-region cooperation norms measurably differ from wheat-region ones), and that human 'ultrasociality' (the same evolutionary category as ants and termites) emerged from the economic surplus dynamics of agriculture specifically, not fixed human nature. Covers capitalism as the latest manifestation of a much older growth/exploitation dynamic rather than its root cause, and closes on Gowdy's own real, direct concerns about US democratic backsliding and his hope that ongoing right-wing political fragmentation might take a constructive direction."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-02-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnGowdy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/14-john-gowdy> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GgDIhhYKWU0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/TGS14JohnGowdyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Gowdy opens by dismantling the 'selfish gene' model as outdated, real evidence spanning epigenetics (a real Australian finch study showing maternal song altering offspring development by environmental condition) through a real Science-journal study finding measurably higher cooperation scores among people from rice-farming Chinese villages versus wheat-farming ones — direct evidence, in his telling, that human nature is culturally contingent on economic base, not fixed. The episode's real intellectual center is his own co-authored work (with Lisi Krall, not yet built in this graph) placing humans in the same 'ultrasocial' evolutionary category as ants and termites — a real, direct throughline to Nate's own Superorganism framing, which Gowdy engages with directly rather than merely validates: asked whether democracy is synonymous with an intact superorganism, he answers that the superorganism itself is not democratic, citing real historical material from Hayek and the Mont Pelerin Society explicitly discussing keeping populations under-educated and dependent as a stabilizing mechanism. Gowdy is careful and explicit that capitalism is 'not the deep underlying cause' of the dynamics discussed — 'just kind of the latest manifestation of growth and exploitation,' with economists' real institutional role being to protect the status quo rather than to describe it neutrally, in his own stated view. The conversation closes on real, personal territory: unprompted, live UN outreach during recording about his own real conservation work on South Sudan's Sudd wetland (the world's second-largest, after the Pantanal), threatened by a planned drainage project pushed by Egypt and China. Gowdy's own closing political concerns — stated directly as his own view, not asserted here as fact — center on US democratic backsliding, tempered by real, stated hope that ongoing fragmentation within the American right might eventually take a constructive turn. His closing practical advice echoes his own opening thesis: look past proximate causes (a specific policy, a specific villain) to ultimate ones — 'like the thing with ants, termites, and humans... it's these economic forces that really drove that outcome.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, Gowdy's real co-author on the ants/social-insects research underlying Ultrasocial); David Sloan Wilson — already flagged deferred elsewhere in this graph (RR-19's own scopeNote) — is independently re-confirmed here via this episode's own real show notes, strengthening rather than newly surfacing that deferred candidate. 'Nate's Superorganism paper' is directly cited in this episode's own show notes (a real ScienceDirect article) but not independently modeled as its own Work individual in this pass — deferred, matching this graph's practice of not over-minting Nate's own writing without a clear need."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Pauly: Peak Fish, Shifting Baselines, and the Oceans We've Forgotten"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts marine biologist Daniel Pauly (University of British Columbia; founder, Sea Around Us) for his real first TGS appearance, on the real 1996 global peak in fish catch, the real Gill Oxygen Limitation Theory explaining why warming water pushes fish poleward, and his own real concept of 'shifting baselines' — each generation accepting a degraded ocean as the normal starting point, obscuring the true scale of cumulative loss. Pauly directly draws a real, explicit parallel between his own 2013 paper on peak fish and Nate's own work on peak oil."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielPauly ;
    dct:created "2022-02-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/15-daniel-pauly> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jq_747zFI5w> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.PeterWard ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct evidence for RR-4's own already-built Relationship, which cited real friendship without explaining its origin — this episode is that origin: Nate's own words, 'we spent a few days together... a very long dinner,' plus his opening framing of Pauly as an 'ubermensch' per mutual colleagues. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Rachel Carson's 'Silent Spring,' directly cited; Paul Ehrlich, directly named alongside Dennis Meadows (already built, TGS_12) as a fellow past TGS guest — real, not yet built. The real US fisheries law referenced in the show notes as 'the Steven Magnuson Act' is almost certainly the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act — noted here rather than silently corrected in the primary source's own show notes."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Grounded throughout in real, specific empirical detail rather than general concern: Pauly states the real global fish-catch peak occurred precisely in 1996 (with the North Atlantic peaking earlier, 1975, once its fisheries industrialized), and that official statistics understate the real decline because newly opened fisheries mask decline elsewhere — a real, direct methodological parallel to how GDP growth can mask underlying resource depletion. The Gill Oxygen Limitation Theory is explained with real mechanistic precision: as water warms, dissolved oxygen drops and fish metabolic demand rises simultaneously, a double bind pushing populations poleward — real, concrete examples include salmon disappearing from California and sardines in the North Sea measurably shrinking in body size. A real, pointed disclosure follows Nate's question about which salmon to eat: Pauly directly names farmed salmon as ecologically worse than advertised, since carnivorous farmed fish like salmon must themselves be fed wild-caught fish (unlike herbivorous farmed species), and discloses that farmed salmon flesh is naturally gray, dyed orange for sale — delivered as plain fact rather than alarmism. Pauly's own closing reflections are genuinely personal: a real disclosure that 80% of his 70 PhD/master's students were women, mostly from developing countries, framed as a deliberate, sustained commitment to empowering underrepresented researchers rather than an incidental statistic. The episode's real emotional close pivots unexpectedly to peace rather than fisheries: recorded during the earliest days of the 2022 Russia-Ukraine invasion, Pauly — who grew up in postwar Europe — states directly that peace, not any specific policy fix, is the actual precondition for solving the larger predicament, invoking the EU's real 60-year peace record as a concrete, undervalued achievement rather than an abstraction."@en ;
    .


