tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aza Raskin: \"AI, The Shape of Language, and Earth's Species\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Aza Raskin, co-founder of the Earth Species Project and Center for Humane Technology (both already built), opening with a real, personal story from Raskin's own time studying forest elephants with the Bayaka people in the Congo rainforest — describing a real, spontaneous group song breaking out in the back of a truck, offered directly as evidence that human communication is 'the full spectrum of human experience,' not language alone. Raskin gives a real, accessible overview of how modern AI actually represents meaning — geometric vector spaces where concepts from different human languages rotate to align with one another — as the direct technical foundation for his own team's real, ambitious goal: using the same techniques to work toward translating non-human animal communication."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-05-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-06-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AzaRaskin ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/22-aza-raskin> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS22_AzaRaskin.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/TGS22_AzaRaskin.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich, tgs:Organization.EarthSpeciesProject, tgs:Organization.CenterForHumaneTechnology ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Raskin gives a real, direct account of his own prior work co-designing Facebook's 'meaningful social interaction' engagement algorithm, describing how optimizing purely for engagement reliably surfaced content triggering anger toward out-groups — a real, firsthand explanation for a dynamic Jonathan Haidt (already built) has separately written about. A real, striking statistic anchors his warning about AI companionship: roughly a quarter of users of the Chinese AI companion app Xiaoice reported telling it 'I love you,' offered as real, early evidence that synthetic relationships can trigger genuine emotional attachment at scale. Raskin's own real, central worry, offered directly when asked what concerns him most: humanity's accelerating capacity to 'reverse-engineer' its own mind — proposing this recursive self-modification loop as a possible real answer to the Fermi Paradox, where sufficiently advanced civilizations turn their own intelligence back on themselves and become caught in a destabilizing feedback loop before ever making contact with others. His own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is psychological rather than technological: helping people let go of the insecurities he believes drive most human bad behavior, since insecurity collapses the space between stimulus and response that genuine reflection requires. Closes with a real, favorite concept borrowed from musician and producer Brian Eno: 'scenius' — genius understood as a property of a whole interconnected scene of people rather than any single individual, offered as the right lens for tackling problems too large for any one human brain to hold."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Daniel Schmachtenberger's real first TGS appearance (#5, directly cross-referenced alongside his second, #20 — already built) remains unbuilt; Jeremy Howard (real, directly cited AI researcher and entrepreneur, not built); Ari Friedlaender and Valeria Vergara (real, directly cited marine-mammal bioacoustics researchers, not built)."@en ;
    .


