tgs:Interview.TGS_73_JoslinFaithKehdy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Joslin Faith Kehdy: \"Lebanon – Beyond Resilience\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Joslin Faith Kehdy, founder of Recycle Lebanon (already built), framed directly as a real, lived preview of what he calls The Great Simplification: Lebanon's real economy contracted by 50% and its currency lost 98% of its value between 2020 and this recording, with roughly three-quarters of the population now in poverty. Kehdy opens with a genuinely dramatic real biography — conceived in Lebanon during its civil war, born in the US specifically to secure American citizenship, raised bilingually in the same rural village (Baskinta) she still lives in, before a childhood stint in America where her own grandmother forbade her from speaking Arabic or French, her actual mother tongues."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-05-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JoslinFaithKehdy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/73-joslin-faith-kehdy> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S1iAa9kP80Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/TGS73JoslinFaithKehdyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.RecycleLebanon ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal turning point anchors Kehdy's own environmental radicalization: after seven years living in Hawaii, a period studying Mandarin in Guangzhou, China left her genuinely anxious and unsettled by its real, direct environmental costs — fast fashion, industrial food production — concluding 'America had softened me' and prompting her real, permanent return to Lebanon in 2011, several years before the country's 2015 waste crisis and 2020 economic collapse. She's real and direct that Lebanon's corruption is not incidental but structural: 'our country does not function without corruption... specifically been designed for the corruption so that the elite politicians and mafia can run the system... into the ground,' with ordinary citizens broadly aware of and resigned to this reality rather than surprised by it. Real, direct personal resolve closes the conversation's substantive core: Kehdy is explicit that she doesn't want hope offered to her — 'I want action, I want reality, I want hands on the ground' — arguing that in genuine crisis, philosophizing about hope and faith becomes a real, dangerous form of delay, and that interconnection and mutual aid, not individual optimism, are what actually determine whether a community survives collapse. Closes on a real, warm, human note distinct from the conversation's gravity: asked what she'll have for dinner that evening with her father, Kehdy describes real, specific preserved sardines her father caught and cured himself, alongside bread and hummus — a small, concrete, grounding detail after an otherwise heavy conversation."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic (Lebanese economic crisis reporting, Recycle Lebanon's own project pages) rather than pointing to further individuals — no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced here."@en ;
    .


