tgs:Interview.TGS_75_KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker: \"Looking Back Towards a Human Powered Future\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with low-tech researcher Kris De Decker (Low-Tech Magazine, Barcelona), a deliberate deeper dive into five categories of appropriate technology — real, concrete alternatives to high-tech defaults for heating, cooling, transportation, and more. Opens with Nate's own real framing for why this matters: the show's usual concepts land cognitively but not emotionally, since 'we're not forced to do it yet' — directly citing a real prior conversation with someone in Lebanon (a 50% GDP collapse) already living the reduced-consumption future most listeners can only imagine. De Decker's central, vivid reframing treats human muscle power as a real, legitimate energy source in its own right — comparable to roughly one square meter of solar panel on a sunny day, but unlike solar, available on demand: 'humans are base load,' in Nate's own memorable phrase."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KrisDeDecker ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/75-kris-de-decker> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4MYqRvm7vX4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS75KrisDeDeckerTranscript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal demonstration grounds the abstract argument: De Decker runs a real hybrid solar/human-power system in his own Barcelona apartment, using a bike generator (built around a wind-turbine-style charge controller) to shrink the battery storage his system needs — battery costs and embodied energy typically account for 70-80% of an off-grid system's total burden. He's candid that combining human and renewable power isn't really about the electricity itself, but about the psychological shift it forces: producing your own power makes you 'think twice about how much power you need' in a way that supply-side renewable-energy discourse alone never does. The conversation's real emotional throughline is De Decker's own repeated argument that most needed change is social, not technical — 'communal household tasks' (shared kitchens, laundries, bathhouses, modeled on real historical precedents from Roman bathhouses to Soviet communal apartments) are framed explicitly as 'social innovation instead of technological innovation,' requiring no new components, just a different mindset about what's normal to do alone versus together. A real, extended detour into US healthcare closes the conversation on a genuinely provocative note: De Decker argues a substantial share of modern medical resource use (single-use plastics, energy-intensive sterilization, aggressive treatment of otherwise self-limiting conditions) reflects profit incentives and defensive practice rather than health necessity, citing a real case study suggesting some historical, less-resource-intensive medical practices produced comparable outcomes. The episode closes on real, unscripted warmth — Nate half-jokingly proposes he'd move into a De Decker-branded communal apartment building 'as long as they allowed dogs' — before De Decker previews a real, specific future research direction: an in-progress piece on the history of steel production and the real difficulty of 'escaping the Iron Age' given how much supposedly sustainable technology still depends on it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joslin Faith Kehdy (real TGS episode #73, the Lebanon-based sustainable-packaging guest Nate references directly and calls 'my friend'), now built (2026-09-02); Robert Lustig, now built (2026-09-02); John Kitzhaber is also now built. DJ White and Simon Michaux are both directly cited by name, but only their real panel appearances (RR-11/RR-24 for White; RR-1 for Michaux) are currently built in this graph — their own real solo episodes (TGS #51 for White; #19 and #49 for Michaux) remain unbuilt, so dct:references was deliberately not asserted here rather than pointing at individuals that don't yet exist."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_76_DanielZetah a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Zetah: \"Regenerative Agriculture and Personal Sovereignty\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, geographically nearby friend Daniel Zetah — living just west of Minneapolis, close to Nate himself — who farms with his wife Stephanie on the family farm where they grow roughly 80% of their own calories. Nate directly frames this as 'a new direction' for the show, deliberately shifting toward practical response rather than only diagnosis, introducing Zetah's own real, coined concept of 'personal sovereignty' as a genuine, livable alternative to conventional systemic dependence."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielZetah ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/76-daniel-zetah> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PtW_Fh9fViw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS76DanielZetahTranscript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems, tgs:Work.Ishmael ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Zetah directly grounds his own real critique of industrial agriculture in stark soil-carbon numbers: pre-settlement Midwest soils held roughly 12-15% organic carbon, now down to under 2%, with regenerative practices capable of rebuilding roughly 0.75-1% carbon content per year — a real, concrete, measurable path back, not merely a qualitative aspiration. He's real and direct that his own real, unconventional life path — 15 years living abroad, including stints as a snake relocator and chemical-spraying auditor in Tasmania, before real, direct time in an off-grid Amish-adjacent community — was itself the necessary preparation for genuinely understanding what 'personal sovereignty' requires in practice, not something he arrived at through theory alone. A real, direct rebuttal to the common ecological-guilt narrative around cattle closes the animal-agriculture section: Zetah argues regeneratively-raised cows produce measurably less methane than CAFO-raised cattle and can function as real, direct carbon-sequestration tools when managed in a genuinely regenerative rotational system, rather than being an inherent environmental liability. Closes with real, direct, unfiltered emotional urgency rather than hedged messaging: Zetah states plainly that 'the time for playing around is over,' directly naming widespread unhappiness within the very system most people continue participating in, before his own real, unguarded answer on what he cares about most — 'life in general' — and his own real, direct fear: not extinction itself, but humanity finding a way to technologically persist inside a genuinely diminished, biodiversity-stripped world."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (soil carbon studies, CAFO reporting, indigenous food systems) rather than pointing to further individuals — Robert Lustig and Joslin Faith Kehdy are both directly cross-referenced and already built elsewhere in this graph, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_77_KateRaworth a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kate Raworth: \"The Superorganism V. The Doughnut\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real friend Kate Raworth, creator of Doughnut Economics, exploring whether a holistic economic framework respecting both social sufficiency and planetary boundaries can genuinely counter the growth-driven pull of what Nate calls the economic Superorganism. Raworth directly names conventional economics' central failure: treating GDP growth as the primary measure of success has produced real, measurable environmental overshoot while simultaneously failing to meet basic human needs for a large share of the world's population."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KateRaworth ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/77-kate-raworth> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7h6X7WBANKY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS78_KateRaworth.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_3_UnlearningEconomics, tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Raworth directly critiques the foundational economic assumption of 'homo economicus' — a rational, self-interested individual optimizer — arguing humans are actually among the most social of all mammal species, making the standard supply-and-demand model a genuinely flawed starting assumption baked into conventional economics from its own foundations. Real, concrete municipal examples ground her argument that Doughnut Economics is already moving from theory to practice: Amsterdam's real, direct adoption of circular-economy policy at the city level, and Grenoble, France's own real, similar municipal initiative, both offered as evidence the framework already has genuine institutional traction. She directly credits Elinor Ostrom's real, Nobel Prize-winning work on commons governance as a real, direct rebuttal to the 'tragedy of the commons' framing, arguing Ostrom's own real research documents genuinely successful long-term collective resource management, not inevitable collective failure. A real, vivid analogy grounds her closing 'finance' preview: comparing standard financial return expectations to a squirrel expecting to find 15% more nuts than it buried the previous autumn, directly questioning why compound financial growth is treated as a reasonable baseline expectation rather than a real, structural design choice worth interrogating. She's real and direct that even successful decoupling stories have limits: Norway's real, genuine success scaling electric vehicle adoption has had no measurable impact on the country's own oil production or exports, a real, direct cautionary example against assuming green technology adoption alone resolves extraction-based economics. Her own real, unhedged magic-wand answer closes the episode: helping every person genuinely feel humanity's interdependence with the whole of Earth's living systems, arguing this single shift in felt understanding, more than any specific policy, is the deepest lever for redesigning economic systems around genuine belonging rather than extraction."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, valuable finds surfaced directly by Raworth herself for a real, proposed future Reality Roundtable on finance: Steve Keen and Jon Erickson, both already independently flagged as real, deferred citations elsewhere in this graph's own tracking — this episode reconfirms both as genuine, guest-suggested priorities, not just citation-frequency artifacts. Josh Farley (real, directly named alongside them, not previously flagged, not built). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Donella Meadows (real, directly cited, Thinking in Systems, not built); Marjorie Kelly (real, directly cited, regenerative ownership, not built)."@en ;
    .


