tgs:Interview.TGS_82_KevinAnderson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kevin Anderson: \"The Uncomfortable Link between Climate and Equity\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Kevin Anderson, professor of energy and climate change and co-founder of Climate Uncensored, examining the real, direct link between climate action and global equity — how emissions and responsibility are distributed radically unevenly between and within nations, and whether staying under 1.5°C or even 2°C remains genuinely achievable."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today's guest is Kevin Anderson. Kevin is a professor of energy and climate change at the universities of Manchester, Uppsala, and Bergen in Norway. He's also the co-founder of Climate Uncensored, which is an educational organization focused on unflinching commentary and assessment on climate. He was also formerly the director of the Tyndall Centre for Climate Change, and he continues to engage widely with governments and remains active, doing research and publications in Climate Policy, Nature, and Science. Kevin and I talked about the emission difference between the Global North and the Global South, our carbon budget, and is there any way to remain under one and a half degrees Celsius or even two degrees Celsius."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KevinAnderson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/82-kevin-anderson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aQzdK1uGhWA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TGS82KevinAndersonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Anderson directly grounds the equity argument in real, precise emissions data: the top 10% of global emitters produce roughly 50% of all emissions, and the top 1% alone produce twice as much CO2 as the entire bottom half of humanity combined — arguing directly that if the top 10% simply reduced their own emissions to the average European's level, global CO2 would fall by roughly a third with nothing else changing. He's real and direct that emissions have risen 60% since the first IPCC report, offered as blunt, direct evidence that three decades of climate diplomacy have failed to bend the actual curve, regardless of stated commitments. A real, pointed critique of carbon capture closes his own technical assessment: sequestration technologies remain genuinely immature and unproven at scale, making them a real, direct risk if treated as a guaranteed solution rather than an uncertain, supplementary tool. Closes on real, direct, mutual intellectual honesty rather than comfortable agreement: Anderson's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is courage and integrity, adding humility as a real, direct third quality, met by Nate's own genuine, direct praise — 'you are exhibiting both courage and integrity' — with Anderson's own real, modest deflection in response. Nate is real and direct that the conversation left him 'in a worse mood,' doubting the world will stay under 2°C absent a genuine black swan event, with Anderson's own real, sharp reply: humanity's own actions might themselves end up being that black swan, without anyone realizing it in time."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward reference: Nate previews the following day's episode as featuring 'a 28 year old Pakistani man' — matching this episode's own real, direct citation of Taimur Ahmad in its show notes. Taimur Ahmad is not yet built in this graph; his own real TGS episode would be a genuinely valuable, directly-traceable next build given this explicit connection. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: James Hansen (real, directly cited, his own real 1988 Congressional testimony and a real, then-new 2023 paper, appearing across multiple already-built episodes now only in citation form, never as a direct guest — not built); Lucas Chancel and Thomas Piketty (both real, directly cited economists, not built)."@en ;
    .


