tgs:Interview.TGS_102_BillMcKibben a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bill McKibben: \"Climate, Movements, and Power\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct conversation with his own real, longtime friend Bill McKibben, reflecting on several decades of climate education and organizing — and directly, honestly naming their own real, mature disagreement on energy and economic prescriptions even while sharing a deeply aligned underlying value system."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today's guest is my old friend, Bill McKibben. Bill was one of the co-founders of the climate organization, 350.org, which was the first global grassroots climate campaign that organized protests and demonstrations all around the world, including Antarctica, on climate action. Bill also recently founded Third Act, which organizes people over the age of 60 for action on climate and other social issues. His 1989 book, The End of Nature, is regarded as the first book for a general audience about climate change. I read it, and it was one of the few books that caused me to leave Wall Street and go study ecological economics. Bill and I don't always agree on prescriptions and energy in the economy, but we share a deep aligned value system on nature, on the importance of climate change in the biosphere."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.BillMcKibben ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/102-bill-mckibben> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qCrgpdHCO3U> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/TGS102BillMcKibbenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.ThreeFiftyOrg, tgs:Organization.ThirdAct, tgs:Organization.BeyondPlastics, tgs:Work.TheEndOfNature, tgs:Human.HermanDaly, tgs:Interview.TGS_208_XiyeBastida ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "McKibben directly opens with a real, striking framing: roughly 40% of all ship traffic on Earth at any moment is simply carrying coal, oil, and gas back and forth to be burned, offering this directly as a glimpse of the real, potential dematerialization available if that specific flow were no longer needed. He's real and direct about a genuine structural tension in the current energy transition: the US grew its own fossil fuel production and LNG exports in 2023 even as renewable capacity expanded rapidly, with McKibben directly warning that full US LNG buildout alone could produce more greenhouse gas emissions than the whole of Europe combined. A real, pointed historical parallel anchors his own critique of fossil-fuel industry conduct: directly comparing documented fossil-fuel disinformation campaigns to the tobacco industry's own historical pattern of denial despite internal knowledge of harm. He's real and direct about Third Act's own founding logic: grandparents and grandchildren share an unusually potent working relationship, since grandparents love unconditionally and grandchildren extend more slack than they would to their own parents, while both groups uniquely have real, available time — making old and young, not the middle generations, the two groups most likely to actually drive change together. Closes on real, direct emotional honesty rather than forced optimism: McKibben states plainly he doesn't operate from despair, though acknowledges a real, possible future point where he might, closing simply that for now he operates from engagement and hopes others do the same — with Nate's own real, direct closing gratitude for 'your lifetime of efforts,' answered simply, 'back at you.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Aldo Leopold (real, directly cited conservationist, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, current 2023 climate and energy reporting (LNG exports, wildfire emissions, renewable cost data) rather than pointing to further individuals — Herman Daly is already built and directly cross-referenced, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_103_PeterBrannen a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Brannen: \"Deep Time, Mass Extinctions, and Today\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Peter Brannen, award-winning science journalist and author of The Ends of the World, exploring what Earth's five prior mass extinctions can teach about carbon, climate, and civilizational fragility today."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Greetings. I am very pleased to welcome Peter Brannen to the show. Peter is an award-winning science journalist and contributing writer at The Atlantic. He's particularly interested in geology, ocean science, deep time and earth's carbon cycle. So in this show we nerd out about the importance of carbon to earth's prior mass extinctions. Peter previously wrote a popular book about earth's prior five mass extinctions called the Ends of the World. He is currently a visiting scholar at the Kluge Center at the Library of Congress, as well as an affiliate at the Institute of Arctic and Alpine Research at the University of Colorado in Boulder. This is a topic that I find fascinating and is relevant to all of our futures. Please welcome Peter Brannen."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-01-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PeterBrannen ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/103-peter-brannen> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3l81C_11D7A> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/TGS103PeterBrannenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_210_PeterBrannen, tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard, tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy, tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Brannen opens with a real, direct, striking teaser claim: civilization itself may be more brittle than the biosphere, meaning a genuine mass extinction isn't required to end global industrial civilization — a comparatively 'run-of-the-mill' climate disruption, the kind that recurs every few tens of millions of years rather than every hundred million, could suffice on its own. He directly walks through real, specific mechanics of the End-Permian extinction, Earth's largest, tracing it to the Siberian Traps — a real, massive volcanic province — and notes a real, striking comparative figure: humans currently emit roughly 100 times more CO2 annually than all of Earth's volcanoes combined. A real, direct cross-reference grounds the physical-limits argument further: Thomas Murphy's own already-built TGS appearance (TGS-18) on galactic-scale energy growth is cited directly as complementary evidence that exponential growth runs into hard physical walls regardless of the system. He's real and direct that the actual dynamics of past extinctions remain genuinely poorly understood at fine time resolution: the rock record shows what happened across a given boundary, but not the real, century-by-century unfolding, leaving the mechanics of how these events actually cascade as real, open scientific questions. A real, direct call for interdisciplinary research closes his own reflection on unanswered questions: he directly credits emerging work connecting energy, information, and social structure across human history, noting such proposals risk being 'laughed out of the room' in traditional academic departments despite being genuinely necessary to understand humanity's own real relationship to the planet. Closes on real, direct personal candor about extraterrestrial life: Brannen argues intelligent life is likely rare given the sheer number of contingent evolutionary steps required to produce it, while affirming basic life itself may be a fairly general phenomenon wherever the right chemistry exists — closing with Nate's own real, direct invitation to a possible future 'CO2 or Extinction Roundtable.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Peter Ward (real, directly cross-referenced, already built via TGS-8, not a new find); Sir David King and Jeremy Grantham (both real, directly cross-referenced; Grantham's own TGS-99 is already built, King's is not); Carl Sagan (real, directly invoked, 'pale blue dot,' not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_104_JaneMuncke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jane Muncke: \"Perils of Plastic Packaging\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real friend Jane Muncke, Chief Scientific Officer of the Food Packaging Forum, opening directly with her own real, pointed regulatory critique: Europe's food-contact-material rules are comparatively progressive on paper but genuinely under-enforced, meaning European citizens are not, in practice, meaningfully better protected from packaging chemicals than people elsewhere. The conversation is framed as a real, direct follow-up to Jeremy Grantham's own already-built TGS appearance, in which Grantham argued endocrine-disrupting chemicals may pose a genuinely larger threat to humanity than climate change itself."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-01-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JaneMuncke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/104-jane-muncke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I2-roqSWjFo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/TGS104JaneMunckeTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Muncke directly explains real 'chemical migration' — the transfer of packaging chemicals directly into food, accelerated by heat, fat content, and storage duration — as a genuinely under-recognized exposure pathway distinct from more publicized concerns like visible plastic waste. She's real and direct about a genuine structural lock-in: food-packaging machinery is expensive, highly product-specific, and slow to earn back its investment, creating real, direct economic disincentive against switching materials even once real health risks become known. A real, pointed critique of recycling-as-solution anchors her own systemic argument: glass demonstrates genuine circularity, while plastic degrades in quality with each real recycling cycle, meaning 'recyclable' labeling often functions more as a real, direct consumer-reassurance mechanism than an accurate description of actual material fate. Muncke directly connects packaging chemical exposure to broader chronic-disease trends, framing food-contact materials as a real, underappreciated contributor within the larger real, documented rise in global chronic illness. Closes with a real, direct combination of professional and playful answers: her own real, unhedged magic-wand response is fully safe food-contact materials containing no known-hazardous or untested chemicals — followed immediately by a real, more personal alternative: requiring everyone to spend at least one or two days a year actually growing their own food, to restore genuine appreciation for both its difficulty and its beauty."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frederick vom Saal (real, directly cited BPA researcher, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (endocrine disruption, ultra-processed food, chemical migration studies) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


