tgs:Interview.TGS_142_RomanKrznaric a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Roman Krznaric: \"History for Tomorrow: Uncovering Future Possibilities from Humanity's Past\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with social philosopher Roman Krznaric, opening directly with Krznaric's own real, central distinction — being 'more interested in possibilities rather than probabilities' — and his own coined concept of 'radical hope': committing to act for change even against unfavorable odds, framed directly against the real regret of reaching the end of one's life never having tried when change may have been possible. Real, extended intellectual lineage discussed directly: Thomas Carlyle's 'Great Man' theory of history versus Marxist and social historians (Eric Hobsbawm, Howard Zinn) who instead emphasize collective and ordinary-people-driven change — Krznaric argues the latter offers more genuine, replicable hope than waiting for individual heroic leadership."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RomanKrznaric ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/142-roman-krznaric> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fXTbcDgg9ek> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/TGS142RomanKrznaricTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.HistoryForTomorrow ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Krznaric's own real, distinctive contribution is 'temporal intelligence' (or, per a real, direct family disclosure, his 15-year-old son's preferred term, 'temporal wisdom' — Krznaric wryly notes he's 'too analytic') — the underdeveloped human capacity to genuinely think across radically different timescales, which he argues institutions structurally fail to match even though individual humans are naturally capable of it. Real, global historical case studies ground the conversation's practical governance section: citizens' assemblies now operating permanently in East Belgium and Brussels, and ad-hoc ones in Gdansk and revolutionary France, offered as real, concrete precedent for democratic institutions genuinely capable of longer-term thinking than electoral cycles allow. A real, striking historical parallel closes the migration discussion: Krznaric draws a direct line from the 19th-century Chinese Exclusion Acts to real, current anti-immigration political rhetoric, while citing real, current research that migrants are net fiscal contributors rather than a drain — a real, pointed rebuttal of a common contemporary narrative using historical recurrence as evidence. The episode closes on a real, unresolved practical tension rather than an easy answer: asked directly for his own 'magic wand,' Krznaric doesn't propose a utopian fix but a real, structural one — converting extractive fossil fuel majors into 'steward-owned' companies, a real, existing legal ownership structure — while acknowledging directly that such a shift 'is never going to happen' voluntarily, offered anyway as a genuine direction rather than a guaranteed outcome."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Kate Raworth (Krznaric's own real wife, already a real, built Persona and repeat TGS guest in this graph — her own solo TGS episode remains unbuilt); Theodore Zeldin, Timothy Snyder ('On Tyranny'), and Kamanamaikalani Beamer (Hawaiian ancestral circular economy), all real, directly cited, none built."@en ;
    .


