tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bill Plotkin: \"Ecological Awakening: A Path Toward Holistic Adulthood\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript. Opens with Plotkin reading David Whyte's poem 'All the True Vows' as a deliberate frame-shift before any conceptual content. Central thesis, stated directly: 'the root cause is arrested human development' — Plotkin's own term for what he elsewhere calls 'systemic human development oppression,' offered as the underlying generator behind the metacrisis rather than one crisis among many. Lays out his ecocentric/soulcentric eight-stage developmental wheel (paired earth/human archetypes per stage: Innocent in the Nest, Explorer in the Garden, Thespian at the Oasis, Wanderer in the Cocoon, Apprentice at the Wellspring, Artisan in the Wild Orchard, Master in the Grove of Elders, Sage in the Mountain Cave) — most contemporary people, he argues, never progress past an unhealthy version of the third stage. Describes ecological awakening directly and experientially, not just conceptually: a felt shift from experiencing an 'invisible wall' between oneself and the rest of the living world to experiencing kinship with it, often accompanied by real grief. Closes on Joanna Macy's 'Great Turning' framework (three dimensions: saving what's left, building new structures, shifting consciousness) as Plotkin's own answer to living with the metacrisis, and a direct, unhedged closing claim: the problem is 'worse than you think, and the solution is closer at hand and easier than you might have feared.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    dct:created "2024-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/146-bill-plotkin> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/TGS146BillPlotkinTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S25UKlLq5m8>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/4u4rGuURrInJOOFPHv5rLu> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening, tgs:Concept.EcocentricDevelopmentalWheel, tgs:Concept.ArrestedHumanDevelopment, tgs:Concept.GreatTurning ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-24: built from the full real transcript (previously a bare stub — number/title/dates only, sourced from 3 independent citations across RR-17, Frankly_132, and TGS_167). Real, well-evidenced Persona candidates cited directly in the transcript body (not just show notes) but deliberately NOT bootstrapped this pass, matching this graph's established deferred-citation discipline: Thomas Berry ('The Dream of the Earth' — direct source for the Sage in the Mountain Cave archetype, per Plotkin's own account of meeting him), Riane Eisler (Dominator vs. Partnership societies — real, no TGS episode of hers built yet despite a real cross-reference in this episode's own show notes), Malidoma Somé (Dagara people), Martín Prechtel (Tzutujil Maya), Joanna Macy (already cited, not yet a Persona), and James Hillman ('we've had a hundred years of psychotherapy and the world's getting worse'). Richard Schwartz and Vanessa Andreotti already existed as Personas in this graph — linked via dct:references, not rebuilt. Gregory Cajete and David Whyte are real but cited more lightly (a poem reading; one book title) — noted here rather than flagged as strong candidates."@en ;
    .


