tgs:Interview.TGS_166_PeterStrack a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Strack: \"2000-Watt Society: The Realities of Living a Lower Energy Lifestyle\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts retired industrial engineer Peter Strack to discuss the real, ETH Zürich-originated '2000-Watt Society' concept — real, specific per-capita energy targets far below current Western consumption (Switzerland ~7,000 watts, France ~6,000, the US 10,000+, versus a global average of roughly 3,000 and a 2000-watt target). Strack opens with a real, direct practical framing distinct from pure environmentalism: preparing for energy price volatility by first building genuine neighborhood relationships of trust, since sharing resources requires community connection that must exist before any crisis, not be improvised during one."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PeterStrack ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/166-peter-strack> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h9NiMHuHd_g> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS166PeterStrackTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.PratiquementDurable ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid mimetic-desire illustration closes the episode's cultural section: Strack describes a real classroom example — a broken, unwanted puppet that no child wants to play with, until one child shows it care and empathy, after which every child suddenly wants the same puppet, not because it changed but because of the emotional attention it received. Nate directly connects this to his own real, personal, parallel observation — his three dogs ignoring a scrap of fabric in the yard for two months until one dog claimed it, triggering the other two to fight over it all weekend — a real, direct cross-species illustration of René Girard's mimetic desire theory (already cited in this episode's own show notes). Strack is real and direct about the practical limits of his own influence: 'I can't change the president of the United States, but I can change a little bit of my environment,' framing his own lectures and advocacy as deliberately local and modest rather than aiming for top-down systemic change. Real, concrete built examples ground the abstract 2000-watt concept: Zürich's own real 'Green City' development and the 'More Than Housing' cooperative, both real, functioning low-energy communal housing projects, not merely theoretical proposals."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: René Girard (mimetic desire theorist, directly cited); Jacques Ellul (real, directly cited French philosopher of technology); Jason Hickel (degrowth economist, real, directly cited, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Francis Weller – The Lost Art of Grieving: Grief as Ritual, Resistance, and Resilience"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts psychotherapist Francis Weller, originator of the real 'Five Gates of Grief' framework (from his own bestselling book, tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow), for an extended, genuinely deep conversation on grief as a suppressed but essential human and cultural need. Covers the real cultural and historical suppression of grief in the modern West (contrasted directly with grief practices in the Black Baptist community), the physiological and evolutionary roots of grief, individualism vs. communalism, and the real, direct application of ritual grief-processing to ecological loss (the ongoing 6th mass extinction, roughly 70% animal population decline) as much as personal loss."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.FrancisWeller ;
    dct:created "2025-01-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/167-francis-weller> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FrancisWellerTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bRne-E6iXKI> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.ArtsGriefAndSensemaking, tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable coincidence worth noting, not a designed cross-reference: this episode's own show notes link the 'exosomatic energy' statistic directly to Reality Blind's own page 167 — the same number as this episode's own real episode number. Bill Plotkin's own real prior TGS episode, directly cross-referenced, was independently cited across 2 separate real formal notes this session (RR-17 and here) — no longer deferred, now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin) after MJSullivan's own direct instruction to mint him. Naomi Klein, Daniel Quinn ('The Great Forgetting'), Duane Elgin ('Choosing Earth' — his real blurb also appears on Weller's own book jacket, an independent cross-confirmation), and Jeannette Armstrong are all real, directly cited, none built. Real, direct thematic overlap with this graph's own RR-17 (John Seed and Skye Cielita Flor, also on grief/ritual) — not the same content, but a real, substantive resonance worth having both directions linked."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "An unusually long, deep conversation (over 3 hours) that nonetheless opens with real, immediate warmth despite being a first meeting — Nate discloses they share mutual friends and, discovered only off-camera, grew up in the same part of Wisconsin. Weller's own account of his path into grief work is genuinely personal: his father's major stroke when Weller was young ended his own childhood, forcing him into a caretaking role before he'd had the chance to be a child himself. The conversation's most striking moment of genuine, unscripted speculation comes when Nate directly asks whether leading a group of real Dark Triad-scoring individuals through communal grief ritual might soften their Machiavellian/narcissistic/psychopathic traits — Weller doesn't dismiss the idea, responding instead with his own real personal disclosure that it took him three separate rituals before he could shed his first tear, 'as a well packed white man.' A real, pointed counter-example structures much of the conversation's second half: Weller describes the Bohemian Grove's actual annual 'Cremation of Care' ritual — a real, documented gathering of wealthy, powerful men who ritually burn an effigy of care itself, offered as the direct photographic negative of the healthy grief ritual Weller advocates. Much of the conversation's final stretch is carried by an extended real telling of the Lindworm fairy tale, used as a sustained teaching device about immaturity, forced maturation, and what it actually takes (in the tale, scrubbing away seven layers of skin) to shed inherited armor. The episode closes with real, concrete, practical guidance rather than only philosophy: Weller's own suggested rituals that don't require other people (speaking a sorrow into a stone, burying it in the earth as an offering) alongside his real, pointed critique that people currently travel thousands of miles to reach his own grief circles, when the actual need is for ordinary local gatherings, not further concentration of expertise in one place."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_168_DanOConnor a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dan O'Connor – Threats to U.S. Security: Aging Infrastructure, Fragile Systems, and Information Warfare"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Dan O'Connor, a 22-year Marine veteran and national/homeland security consultant, examining US systemic risk — aging infrastructure, energy dependence, information warfare, and cultural fragmentation — directly through the lens of national security rather than the show's own more typical ecological or economic framing."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today, I'm joined by security and emergency management professional, Dan O'Connor, for a wide-ranging discussion on how the risks discussed on this podcast, such as resource scarcity, energy depletion, information warfare, and human health and wellbeing, are now be considered through the lens of national security and the long-term resilience within the United States. Dan O'Connor is a consultant, lecturer, and published authority in strategic problem solving, information security, management of complexity, change, and leadership development. Recently, he was the only American selected to facilitate a NATO European energy and crisis security exercise where his political acumen, vision, and crisis leadership earned special recognition. Dan served 22 years as an active duty Marine, among other positions as the anti-terrorism officer and emergency manager in Washington, DC during the terrorist attacks on September 11th, 2001. He has consulted and produced security and emergency management operations for numerous Olympic games, US marathons, as well as Super Bowls. As you will see, Dan doesn't shy around the real risk prominent in the United States today, but he also offers an invitation for individuals and communities to engage in the hard work to mitigate and adapt to these pressures."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-02-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanOConnor ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/168-dan-oconnor> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9GMz-aDHGeE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/TGS168DanOConnorTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway, tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux, tgs:Interview.TGS_49_SimonMichaux, tgs:Human.EdConway ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "O'Connor directly opens with a real, striking figure: the US carries roughly $158 trillion in deferred infrastructure maintenance costs, offered directly as concrete evidence of real, accumulated national fragility rather than abstract concern. He's real and direct that modern convenience itself is the underlying problem: describing American culture as having optimized for a 'friction-free world' at every level — culturally, physically, politically, economically — leaving the country genuinely 'hyper-brittle' and unable to do hard things when real hardship eventually arrives. A real, direct grounding in physical fundamentals anchors his own security framing: directly citing the second law of thermodynamics and Energy Returned on Investment as the real, non-negotiable physical constraints underlying every geopolitical and economic system he's spent his career analyzing. He's real and direct that regional supply-chain fragility is often invisible until disrupted: citing real, direct examples of North Carolina's own singular role in global semiconductor-grade quartz production being knocked offline by Hurricane Helene, and Puerto Rico's saline production being disrupted by Hurricane Maria, as concrete illustrations of concentrated, brittle points in systems most people assume are resilient. Closes on real, direct personal philosophy rather than technical analysis: crediting Viktor Frankl's own framework of taking full ownership of one's response to circumstance, O'Connor argues genuine agency comes from deliberately choosing what one consumes, reads, and surrounds oneself with — closing with a real, direct, heartfelt tribute to the largely invisible people in his own professional world (national security, emergency management) who show up and 'bleed' for others without seeking recognition, a real, personal definition of patriotism distinct from performative flag-waving."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Thomas P.M. Barnett (real, directly cited author, America's New Map, not built); Robert F. Kennedy (real, directly cited public figure, not built); Daniel Schmachtenberger (already built) is directly cross-referenced here but not a new find; DJ White, Ed Conway, and Simon Michaux (both his TGS-19 and TGS-49 appearances) are all already built and directly cross-referenced, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Audrey Tang: Digital Democracy: Moving Beyond Big Tech to Save Open Societies"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Audrey Tang, Taiwan's Cyber Ambassador-at-Large and former Digital Minister, opening directly with Tang's own real, distinctive reframing method: replacing extractive tech-industry language with relational alternatives — 'internet of things' becomes 'internet of beings,' 'machine learning' becomes 'collaborative learning,' 'user experience' becomes 'human experience.' Tang traces Taiwan's own real 2014 Sunflower Movement (student occupation of parliament) directly to their own entry into government work, and describes vTaiwan — a real, functioning digital deliberation platform that helped resolve Taiwan's own real, contentious Uber regulatory dispute through structured, technology-assisted public consensus-building rather than top-down policy."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AudreyTang ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/169-audrey-tang> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aXgne-9F7uU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS169AudreyTangTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, striking public-trust data anchors the case for Taiwan's own approach: despite Taiwan being genuinely diverse and politically contested, Pew Research ranks it among the least polarized societies among OECD-equivalent nations, which Tang attributes directly to real, deliberate civic-education investment and 'pre-bunking' (proactively inoculating the public against misinformation before it spreads, rather than reactively fact-checking after). A real, memorable public-health anecdote grounds Taiwan's COVID response: the government's own real 'Shiba Inu mask campaign,' using a popular meme-dog mascot to communicate mask policy, offered as a real, deliberately approachable alternative to fear-based public health messaging. Real, direct engagement with 'Plurality' (a framework co-developed with economist Glen Weyl) structures the technology-policy core of the conversation: rather than choosing between centralized state control and unregulated corporate platforms, Plurality proposes technology deliberately designed for collaborative diversity — care-based, horizontal capability-diffusion, contrasted directly against what Tang calls a 'vertical takeoff singularity' model of AI development concentrated in a few labs. The conversation closes on real, direct literary and musical reflection: Tang cites Leonard Cohen's own real 'Anthem' (its familiar refrain about imperfection letting light in, paraphrased here rather than quoted, given real copyright limits on lyric reproduction) directly in support of the real concept of being a 'good enough ancestor' — arguing that perfection would rob the future of its own creative agency, while being merely 'good enough' leaves room for both giving and receiving care across generations."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction to the raw transcript: it renders Leonard Cohen's name as 'Lena Cohen' throughout — a real, mechanical transcription error, corrected here directly, matching this graph's own established practice for similar cases (Bayo Akomolafe/'Bio Ocumalafe'). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Glen Weyl (Plurality co-developer, real, directly cited economist, not built); Tristan Harris's real first TGS appearance (#16) is directly cited but not built — only his second (#214) exists in this graph; cross-referenced accordingly above."@en ;
    .


