tgs:Interview.TGS_173_WillMarshall a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Will Marshall – Planetary Insights: How Satellites Could Transform Our Understanding of Earth's Predicament"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Will Marshall, co-founder and CEO of Planet Labs (already built), exploring how a real, direct constellation of hundreds of satellites imaging the entire planet daily is being used to make deforestation, methane leaks, and other ecological harms visible, accessible, and actionable — directly framed by Nate himself as one of AI's most genuinely positive use cases, 'in service of life.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "I am pleased to be joined today by Will Marshall, who is the co-founder and CEO of Planet Labs, which provides governments, businesses, and research institutions with the highest frequency satellite data, images, and analytics available today, including the photographing of the entire planet at least once per day. He and I discussed the current projects at Planet Labs that are working to improve the health of our earth's ecological systems, from tracking illegal deforestation in the Amazon to identifying methane leaks that might otherwise go undetected coming from fracking operations. Prior to co-founding Planet, Will was a scientist at NASA and the University Space Research Association, where he was a systems engineer on lunar orbiter missions and was the technical lead on research projects in space debris remediation. In my opinion, the data coming from Planet Labs is one of the most positive use cases of artificial intelligence, with the potential to enable an unimaginable number of other projects and initiatives that are in service of life."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.WillMarshall ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/173-will-marshall> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uor09_iZJzw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS173WillMarshallTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.PlanetLabs, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Marshall directly credits the 'overview effect' — the real, documented shift in perspective astronauts report seeing Earth from space — as a genuine motivating force behind Planet's own mission to make planetary-scale change visible to people who will never leave the ground. He's real and direct about Planet's own operational reach: monitoring Brazilian Amazon deforestation directly and in near-real-time, tracking the war in Ukraine, and identifying methane leaks from fracking operations that would otherwise go completely undetected, offered directly as concrete, already-functioning examples rather than future promises. A real, striking framing anchors the episode's own closing arc: Marshall directly names three impending 'Copernicus moments' — discovering non-human life beyond Earth, decoding animal communication (directly crediting Aza Raskin's own already-built work), and creating artificial superintelligence — each one directly decentering humanity's sense of its own uniqueness the way Copernicus once decentered Earth itself from the cosmos. He's real and direct that AI represents the most genuinely dangerous of the three, describing a real, active geopolitical and corporate race dynamic (US, China, OpenAI, Google, Anthropic) where every major actor recognizes the risks yet continues accelerating anyway, for fear of losing an existential competition — directly stating he has 'not seen a good answer' for how humanity navigates this. Closes on real, direct personal candor from Nate: crediting an in-person tour of Planet's own satellite manufacturing facility as 'really eye-opening,' with Marshall's own real, plain closing message — the opportunity to protect the planet is real and urgent, and it requires 'all hands on deck.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Kari Stoever (real, directly cited, Planetary Health Check, her own real TGS episode referenced, not yet built — a real, concrete, traceable next candidate given the direct citation); Al Gore (real, directly cited regarding Climate Trace, a real, well-known public figure cited only in passing, not built); Chris Boshuizen and Robbie Schingler (Marshall's own real, direct Planet Labs co-founders, not built)."@en ;
    .


