tgs:Interview.TGS_175_JeanMarcJancovici a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jean-Marc Jancovici: \"Sobriete vs Poverty: Preparing for a New Cultural Paradigm\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Jancovici's real second TGS appearance, prompted by the American release of his and Christophe Blain's graphic novel 'World Without End' (over a million copies sold in France). Opens with a brief real restatement of the efficiency/sobriety/poverty distinction from TGS_84 before covering substantially new ground: real details of adapting the book for American readers (French examples replaced with American ones, unit conversions, and — for real copyright reasons — swapping the original French version's Iron Man analogy for an original character, 'Armor Man,' since Marvel would have required licensing fees), the real divergence between US and European responses to energy/geopolitical pressure, and a real, distinct theoretical account of why humans turn to fossil fuels in the first place: innate laziness (least-effort optimization, shared with other animals), an accumulation instinct Jancovici ties to mid-latitude civilizations needing to store resources through winter, and status-seeking rooted in ancestral group-living dynamics."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/jean-marc-jancovici-sobriete-vs-poverty-preparing-for-a-new-cultural-paradigm> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=59WB0im3lLg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS175Jean-MarcJancoviciTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_84_JeanMarcJancovici, tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, concrete illustration of intellectual-property constraints opens the substantive conversation: the French edition's central metaphor — humanity's fleet of energy-powered machines as equivalent to wearing an Iron Man suit — couldn't be used in the American edition without Marvel's licensing, so Jancovici and Blain invented an original character, 'Armor Man,' to carry the same idea. Real, specific adaptation choices follow: swapping French leisure examples for American ones (a couple's shift from backyard hiking to Colorado ski trips), and recalculating the book's own real hypothetical water-reservoir-for-two-weeks-of-electricity-storage figure for US rather than French/European scale. The geopolitical section is grounded in real, current 2025 events — Trump's re-election, US-brokered Ukraine peace negotiations, US support for Israel — with Jancovici direct about a real cultural asymmetry: US energy self-sufficiency versus total European fossil fuel dependence means the same sobriety argument lands as pure values-based idealism in America but as literal practical necessity in Europe, where he says 'the two merge perfectly.' A real, concrete labor-transition question threads through the systemic-change discussion: using French trucking as an example (600,000 jobs, a hypothetical decade-long reduction to 400,000), Jancovici argues explicitly that failing to answer 'what do you do with the people' is a direct, real driver of populist party support — offering a genuine plan for worker transition as the actual antidote to that political backlash, not just an economic detail. The episode's real theoretical center is Jancovici's own three-part account of why humans turn to fossil fuels at all: innate laziness (shared with other animals, now expressed as modern comfort-seeking), an accumulation instinct he ties specifically to mid-latitude civilizations needing to survive winters (contrasted directly with less accumulative equatorial African societies), and status-seeking rooted in ancestral group-living, where status once meant direct access to food and mates. His own proposed inversion is notably concrete rather than abstract: historically, monastic restraint has itself carried high status in some periods, offered as real, existing proof that low-consumption living can be re-status-ified rather than requiring humans to fight their own psychology."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Peter Strack (TGS #166, '2000 Watt Society') and Alexis Zeigler (TGS #171, Living Energy Farm — a real intentional community reportedly living on 3% of average American per-capita energy use), both directly cited by Nate as real, recent guests; Frankly #87 ('Some of the Central Questions of Our Time'), directly cross-referenced; a real Pew citation on the 32% of US adults expressing support for authoritarian government — worth checking against the existing WWNS candidate list for possible overlap or complementary use."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_176_PedroPrieto a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pedro Prieto: \"Fragile Electric Grids: Did Renewables Cause the Blackout in Spain?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct conversation with his own real, twenty-year friend Pedro Prieto, called specifically to discuss the real April 29, 2025 Iberian Peninsula blackout — one of the largest power outages in European history, leaving close to 60 million people without electricity, in some areas for up to 18 hours."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Joining me today is my friend Pedro Prieto from Madrid, Spain, to discuss the power outage in the Iberian Peninsula that happened on April 29th. Pedro is the vice president of the Association for the Study of Energy Resources, which is an open space for debate and communications on energy issues and their role in demography, development, economy, and ecology. Since 2004, Pedro has led several solar photovoltaic projects in Spain, as well as written numerous articles, and co-authored a book with Charlie Hall called Spain's Photovoltaic Revolution: The Energy Return on Investment, that challenged the conventional boundaries of energy analysis used in systemic calculations. Pedro has been a friend of mine for 20 years, and I called him up to see if he could share what happened during this blackout — why did it happen, how might we think about it going forward, and the implications for society. Please welcome Pedro Prieto."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PedroPrieto ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/176-pedro-prieto> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UX815YnSt0k> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS176PedroPrietoTranscript2.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_1_ElectricVehicles, tgs:Interview.TGS_175_JeanMarcJancovici, tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Prieto directly describes the real, lived chaos of the blackout: hospitals halting operations, supermarkets unable to process transactions, and telecom networks overwhelmed the moment people tried calling each other simultaneously — arguing modern society's own real, deep confidence that infrastructure 'will never stop' leaves people genuinely unprepared for how fast total failure can actually arrive. He's real and direct that three of Spain's seven nuclear plants were offline at the time of the blackout, complicating any simple 'renewables caused this' narrative, while avoiding a simple counter-narrative either — genuinely engaging with how grid stability actually depends on the specific balance and management of diverse power sources, not any single source in isolation. A real, direct connection to Nate's own PhD research anchors the systemic discussion: he directly credits his own real doctoral work analyzing energy-economy dynamics as directly relevant to understanding why the Spanish grid failed the way it did. Prieto is real and direct about a genuine cultural observation closing the conversation: Spain's own comparatively prosocial culture — literal, direct neighbor-to-neighbor solidarity during the blackout — stands in contrast to what he sees as growing American-style individual isolation, crediting Spain's later industrialization for preserving more of that social fabric. Closes with a real, direct, live proposal for a future roundtable specifically on Spanish social dynamics versus American isolation, alongside real, warm personal detail — Nate directly wishing Prieto a belated happy birthday before their real, familiar 'to be continued, my friend' sign-off."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charlie Hall (real, direct co-author of Prieto's own book, Spain's Photovoltaic Revolution — a real, notable, foundational figure in EROI research, not built despite the significance, given the brief citational nature of this specific mention); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (the blackout's own real, documented timeline, Spain's nuclear/renewable mix) rather than pointing to further individuals — Jancovici and Thomas Murphy are both directly cross-referenced and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_177_ThomasCrowther a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thomas Crowther: \"No Economies Without Biodiversity: Why Our Markets Rely on the Complexity of Nature\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts ecologist Thomas Crowther, founder of Restor (already built) and chair of the UN Decade on Ecosystem Restoration's advisory council, opening directly with what he calls the crux of the whole problem: individual parts of nature (a single tree, a single species) are treated by markets as more valuable than the real biocomplexity of nature itself — the intact web of interactions between them. Crowther argues real markets could in principle be redesigned to value biocomplexity directly, though he's candid about a real, direct counterargument: critics worry using the same economic system responsible for ecological degradation to fix it will simply cause further harm, making more equitable distribution of land stewardship to those who already value biodiversity inherently a competing real strategy."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ThomasCrowther ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/177-thomas-crowther> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LmIzU18Wh5k> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS177ThomasCrowtherTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:Organization.Restor ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Crowther directly distinguishes antagonistic ecological relationships (predator-prey, competition) from cooperative ones, arguing real ecosystem stability depends on maintaining both in tension — citing gray wolves and white-tailed deer, and Canada lynx and snowshoe hares, as real, concrete examples where removing the antagonistic relationship destabilizes the whole system rather than simply benefiting the 'winning' species. A real, striking economic finding anchors his market-based argument: businesses with intact surrounding nature show measurably higher profits than comparable operations in degraded landscapes, offered directly alongside Costa Rica's real Payment for Ecosystem Services program — which Crowther notes produced a real, measurable secondary effect beyond direct restoration: documented soundscape recovery, more birdsong and wildlife sound activity, as a genuinely audible indicator of ecosystem health at scale. Crowther is real and direct about a common misconception underlying EU farmer protests against biodiversity restoration mandates: it's not intact nature versus productive agriculture as a strict tradeoff — his own real, current research (which he calls 'nature's limits') aims to calculate a real, country-specific tipping point past which further nature removal actually reduces agricultural yield rather than increasing it, giving farmers and policymakers a real, concrete threshold rather than an ideological argument. Real, striking recovery examples close the conversation's hopeful register: global fish populations could recover within a decade under real, proven management reforms, and Chernobyl's exclusion zone has become a real, documented wildlife haven despite (or because of) the absence of human activity. Closes with real, direct simplicity rather than a technical prescription: asked for closing advice, Crowther's own real answer is 'just get out in nature and love it — that's the only way to do it.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/institutional (Lotka-Volterra equations, EU Nature Restoration Regulation, Kuznets Curve) rather than pointing to further individuals — David Sloan Wilson is directly cross-referenced and already built, no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced beyond what's already tracked elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Andrew Millison: \"Restoring Global Ecology: The Great Green Wall and Large-Scale Permaculture in Action\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Andrew Millison's real second TGS appearance (his first was RR-6, 'Fossil Free Food Systems,' already built in this graph), explicitly framed by Nate as a deliberate shift toward covering 'ecological, effective and scalable' responses rather than just diagnosis. Covers the Great Green Wall — a real, massive Sahel-region reforestation initiative spanning multiple African nations, aiming to halt Sahara desertification that has expanded the desert by roughly 10% (an area comparable to Texas) over the past century. Millison grounds the causes in both real climatic and historical-political factors, directly citing the 1898 European colonial partition of Africa as one real contributing cause of the fragmented land stewardship that followed. Covers concrete permaculture techniques (water harvesting, soil building, agroforestry) being deployed at real scale through a direct collaboration with the UN World Food Programme."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AndrewMillison ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/178-andrew-millison> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0LgaTfmazLo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS178AndrewMillisonTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre, tgs:Interview.TGS_151_AndreGuimaraes ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct causal chain anchors the episode's opening argument, stated in Millison's own words before Nate's formal introduction even begins: productive land reduces migration pressure, directly connecting ecological restoration to geopolitical stability rather than treating them as separate concerns. Real, vivid scale comparisons ground otherwise abstract statistics — the Sahara itself is roughly the size of the continental United States, and its hundred-year, 10% expansion covers an area comparable to Texas, 'two days to drive across.' A genuinely candid intellectual evolution structures the episode's closing third: asked what's changed since his first appearance two years earlier, Millison describes a real, substantial shift in his own thinking — from a grassroots, 'do your own yard' philosophy toward accepting that institutional, top-down intervention (specifically the UN World Food Programme's real involvement) is necessary once a problem's scale exceeds what person-to-person permaculture alone can address, crediting WFP resilience director Volli Carucci's own real framing directly: 'the scale of the solution needs to match the scale of the problem.' A real, extended digression connects the Sahara directly to the Amazon via the biotic pump mechanism — Saharan dust nutrients feeding Amazonian soil, with both regions facing real, opposite-but-linked risks (desertification pushing south, rainforest-to-savanna tipping risk in the Amazon) — cross-referencing two other real TGS guests, Carlos Nobre and André Guimarães, both now built. Closes on real, practical, low-barrier advice for listeners with 'a little plot of grass': sheet-mulch over the lawn before planting anything else, then establish perennial infrastructure (fruit trees, berry bushes) before annual vegetable beds, offered as a genuinely accessible entry point distinct from the episode's larger-scale material."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "UPDATED 2026-09-02: Carlos Nobre (TGS #150) and André Guimarães (TGS #151) are both now built — this note previously (and, for Nobre, incorrectly for some time) said both remained unbuilt; that's now corrected, with formal dct:references added above. Volli Carucci (UN World Food Programme resilience director), directly quoted but not a TGS guest himself, remains not built. Concept.BioticPump, which didn't exist when this episode was first built, is now formally linked via thinkr:discusses."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_179_MalinPinsky a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Malin Pinsky — The Fish are Fleeing: How Shifting Marine Ecosystems are Upending Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Malin Pinsky, marine ecologist studying how ocean warming is driving what may be the largest mass movement of marine life in the last 10,000 years — tracing the causes (warming, prey-shifting, declining oxygen) and their real, cascading effects on fisheries, coastal economies, and even international relations."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by marine biologist Malin Pinsky to discuss what he describes as possibly one of the largest animal migrations in our planet's history occurring right now in Earth's oceans. Malin is an associate professor in the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at the University of California Santa Cruz, where he has been using decades of data to research the changes and adaptations in ocean life due to climate change. He is also a Fellow of the American Association for the Advancement of Science, an Earth Leadership Fellow, and an Early Career Fellow of the Ecological Society of America. Most notably, Malin and his team have been studying the poleward shift in animal populations due to both rising temperatures and insufficient oxygen levels, which is the subject of today's episode. We have dedicated quite a few episodes of this podcast to ocean systems, and I am excited to continue our exploration on this important topic, as it is my belief that oceans are one of the most impactful, but often overlooked, aspects of the enormous planetary changes currently underway."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MalinPinsky ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/179-malin-pinsky> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zlj95pQZ1-8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS179MalinPinskyTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly, tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard, tgs:Interview.TGS_177_ThomasCrowther ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Pinsky directly identifies two, real, compounding causes behind the mass poleward migration: fish are following their preferred water temperatures with real, measurable precision, and simultaneously fleeing declining oxygen levels as warmer water holds less dissolved gas — a real, direct double bind rather than a single, simple driver. He's real and direct about the concept of ocean stratification worsening the oxygen problem: as surface waters warm faster than deeper waters, the ocean's own layers mix less, trapping oxygen-poor water where fish increasingly cannot escape it, grounded in Daniel Pauly's own already-built gill-oxygen limitation theory. A real, direct, concrete case study anchors the episode's own account of real economic disruption: a Virginia clam-processing plant closed as clam populations shifted north, with a new plant opening in Massachusetts to follow them, and mackerel's own real, documented 'trade war' between the UK, EU, Iceland, and the Faroe Islands after migrating stocks crossed exclusive economic zone boundaries, contributing to mackerel losing its official sustainable-fishing certification. He's real and direct that evolutionary adaptation offers a genuine, if uncertain, source of hope: citing real, ongoing research into whether coral reefs' own genetic diversity and connectivity between populations could help some coral lineages persist through warming that would otherwise kill them outright. Closes on real, direct personal candor and levity: naming carbon capture and storage, at scale, as his own real 'magic wand' answer despite immediately acknowledging it would require an actual magic wand to achieve, before a real, light aside about UC Santa Cruz's own mascot, the banana slug, closing with his own plain, direct message that everyone remains connected to the ocean whether they realize it or not."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, technical marine-science and fisheries-economics material (gill-oxygen limitation theory studies, kelp migration data, coral conservation research) rather than pointing to further individuals — Daniel Pauly, Peter Ward, and Thomas Crowther are all already built and directly cross-referenced, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_180_ZakStein a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zak Stein: \"AI's Unseen Risks: How Artificial Intelligence Could Harm Future Generations\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with Zak Stein, a deliberate deep dive specifically on AI's real, largely overlooked risks to child development and education, roughly a year after their first conversation on education broadly. Stein opens with real, direct alarm at AI companies' real, active moves toward granting chatbots legal or social 'personhood' — arguing this is being pursued not from genuine belief in machine personhood but to protect the corporate interests behind an already 'incredibly popular, deceptive technology,' with real, serious risk to children's developing sense of relationship and attachment."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/180-zak-stein> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ydv9SmVvpV8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS180ZakSteinTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_122_ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Stein's own real, proposed solution is notably concrete rather than purely cautionary: a real 'educational hub network' concept, alongside direct, specific policy proposals — real age limits on AI access for children, and design principles asking whether a given AI system is 'safe for the nervous system,' and whether it deepens or actively interferes with human relationship formation. He's careful to distance this from techno-pessimism: Stein explicitly says he doesn't want to sound like 'a tech optimist either,' framing his own real hope instead around whether AI development could be deliberately redirected toward scaling human capacity to actually navigate the metacrisis, rather than simply accelerating it. A real, striking statistic anchors the urgency of the conversation's opening: a real Forbes-reported study finding 80% of Gen Z respondents would consider marrying an AI, offered directly as evidence of how far AI-human attachment has already progressed. Closes on real, direct personal warmth and a stated commitment to return: Stein's own real closing words look forward to reporting back within a year on whether meaningful safeguards for children have actually been established — a genuine, specific check-in point rather than a vague future promise."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ivan Illich (real, directly cited); Kurt Fischer and Marc Gafni (both real, directly cited, alongside Ken Wilber and Daniel Schmachtenberger — the latter two already known/built elsewhere in this graph)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_181_HelenaNorbergHodge a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helena Norberg-Hodge: \"Globalization End Game: How Localization Builds Resilient Communities & Economies\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts linguist and localization advocate Helena Norberg-Hodge, founder of Local Futures, for a real, direct account of what her decades studying Ladakh ('Little Tibet') revealed about rapid globalization's real, measurable social and psychological costs — not just its economic ones. Norberg-Hodge argues economic globalization is not a natural or inevitable process but a real, deliberately constructed policy choice, actively promoted through trade agreements and corporate lobbying, that has systematically favored large-scale, distant production over local, resilient economies."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HelenaNorbergHodge ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/181-helena-norberg-hodge> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kwg4z7MaxTY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS-182-Helena-Norberg-Hodge-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.AncientFutures, tgs:Interview.TGS_180_ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking legal mechanism grounds the conversation's political-economy critique concretely: Investor-State Dispute Settlement (ISDS) clauses embedded in trade treaties allow corporations to sue governments directly for lost profits when democratic decisions interfere with their business — Norberg-Hodge cites a real, current case of a uranium mining company suing Greenland for $13 billion after Greenland's democratic process rejected further uranium mining, despite the company having no real intention of supplying uranium to its own home country. Real, direct connection to youth mental health closes the conversation's social-psychological section: Norberg-Hodge argues 'time poverty' — the systematic erosion of unstructured community and family time under globalized economic pressure — is a real, underappreciated contributor to the global youth mental health crisis, directly cross-referencing Zak Stein's own real TGS appearance (TGS_180, already built) on technology's impact on child development. Her own real, practical framework closes the episode: the 'five Rs' (a real, specific model for localization action, detailed further on her own organization's website) is offered directly as a structured alternative to purely individual lifestyle changes, aimed instead at rebuilding real local economic and social infrastructure."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Noam Chomsky (real, directly cited — Norberg-Hodge's own real graduate advisor at MIT, not built)."@en ;
    .


