tgs:Interview.TGS_184_ConnorLeahy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Connor Leahy — Algorithmic Cancer: Why AI Development Is Not What You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Connor Leahy, AI researcher, entrepreneur, and leading AI-risk advocate, examining whether humanity actually has the technical capability to guide advanced AI development at all — and Leahy's own real, direct concept of 'algorithmic cancer,' AI-generated content that crowds out genuine human creation because the distributing algorithms can't tell the difference."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm pleased to be joined by Connor Leahy, an expert in AI development and a leading advocate for awareness and regulation of the existential risks that unfettered artificial intelligence presents for society and our future. Connor Leahy is the founder and CEO of Conjecture, who is working on aligning artificial intelligence systems by building infrastructure that allows for the creation of scalable, auditable and controllable AIs. Previously, Connor co-founded EleutherAI, which was one of the earliest and most successful open source large language model communities, as well as a home for early discussions on the risks of those same AI systems. If you've ever wondered about some of the basics behind how artificial intelligence software is created, or what exactly about that process makes AI innovation both rapid and risky, this conversation with Connor provides a fantastic primer on these topics, although it is not for the faint of heart. This episode also compliments our previous content on the discussions of AI, including the recent episode with Zak Stein on artificial intelligence and education."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ConnorLeahy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/184-connor-leahy> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7Y_1_RmCJmA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS-184-Connor-Leahy-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.Conjecture, tgs:Organization.EleutherAI, tgs:Organization.ControlAI, tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI, tgs:Interview.TGS_122_ZakStein, tgs:Interview.TGS_180_ZakStein, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Human.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Human.ZakStein, tgs:Human.AudreyTang ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Leahy directly states his own central concern is extinction risk, not job displacement or misinformation: if a more intelligent, non-aligned entity shares the planet with humanity and wants its resources, he argues plainly that the more intelligent party wins, regardless of human intent or preparation. He's real and direct about the cognitive gap already visible between AI and prior benchmarks, drawing a real, direct comparison between chimpanzee and human cognition to illustrate that even a modest jump in general intelligence produced civilization-altering consequences for the less intelligent party, not a gentle transition. A real, direct concrete initiative closes his own account of what's actually achievable: ControlAI's letter-writing and lobbying campaign secured 35 real, sitting parliamentarians as supporters against AI extinction risk despite being told at the outset that no one would engage — offered directly as evidence against the self-fulfilling belief that ordinary people and institutions are powerless to act. He's real and direct that founder psychology in AI labs deserves real scrutiny, citing directly a real, academic framing of 'founder as victim, founder as god' to describe how figures like Sam Altman and Elon Musk are simultaneously mythologized and self-mythologized in ways that obscure ordinary accountability. Closes on real, direct personal candor rather than technical argument: given a real, direct 'magic wand' question, Leahy answers half-jokingly that he'd want a future textbook with AI alignment and moral philosophy already solved and explained simply — before naming his own real, plain, unadorned wish for humanity: not utilitarian maximization, just that 'everyone gets to be okay.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real TGS-71 appearance ('Artificial Intelligence and The Superorganism'), directly cited, not yet built despite Schmachtenberger himself already being a built Human in this graph; Frankly-92 ('In Service of Life?'), Frankly-83 ('Artificial Intelligence and the Lost Ark'), and Frankly-49 ('AI vs. Real Ecology'), all real, directly cited prior Franklys on AI, none yet built; Frankly-96 ('The 8 Faces of AI'), also directly cited here, is built in this same session's batch, resolving that specific gap directly. Geoffrey Hinton (real, directly cited, 'Godfather of AI,' not built)."@en ;
    .


