tgs:Interview.TGS_188_LynAlden a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nothing Can Stop This Train: Our Financial Predicament From a Systems Perspective"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with Lyn Alden, opening with her own real, direct definition of money as fundamentally a ledger — one that can be backed by something scarce like gold, or by shared societal trust as with central banking — arguing when that ledger breaks, it makes economic decision-making genuinely less efficient and pushes people toward defensive, arbitrary value-storage strategies to protect themselves against real, ongoing currency debasement."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-07-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/188-lyn-alden> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LT96QsadYtw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/TGS-188-Lyn-Alden-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Alden directly explains real, structural currency debasement as a distributional choice, not a neutral technical event: when a government runs deficits and its central bank effectively finances them, the resulting debasement has real winners and losers — anyone not directly receiving the deficit spending effectively pays for it, whether they hold that currency domestically or internationally. She's real and direct about the US's own current structural position: running fiscal deficits large relative to GDP even compared to other developed economies, combined with a real, persistent structural trade deficit, both tied directly to the US dollar's global reserve-currency status rather than being separable problems. A real, direct, personally revealing moment closes the disconnect discussion: Alden notes that even finance professionals are genuinely siloed by their own specific jobs and life circumstances, meaning real, systemic blind spots aren't a failure of individual expertise but a structural feature of how specialized knowledge naturally fragments — sometimes creating real investment opportunities, sometimes producing real policy errors. Closes on a real, direct point of respectful disagreement with Nate's own general outlook: Alden states she leans more toward optimism than he does, grounded in her own observation that human ingenuity tends to find 'the next runway' whenever a system hits real friction, pushing timelines further out than pessimistic models tend to predict — while agreeing the resulting disruption remains genuinely real and locally severe even when the largest-scale collapse keeps getting deferred."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely institutional/statistical (Federal Reserve data, Treasury yields, real interest rates) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


