tgs:Interview.TGS_190_StephenJenkinson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Forgotten Skills of Dying and Grieving Well: How Engaging with Loss Can Help Us Live More Fully"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Stephen Jenkinson, cultural activist and former palliative-care counselor, opening with Jenkinson's own real, central observation from hundreds of deathbeds: people don't rise to some better version of themselves when dying — 'the dying turned out to be same old, the dying that people died in the manner of their living.' He argues Anglo-North American culture is not just death-phobic but specifically grief-illiterate, having lost any real cultural repertoire for approaching death as something other than a personal catastrophe to be managed."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.StephenJenkinson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/190-stephen-jenkinson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XgWpq66LuWY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/TGS-190-Stephen-Jenkinson-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.DieWise, tgs:Work.Matrimony, tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Jenkinson's own real, central distinction reframes grief entirely: grief is 'an understanding, not an affliction' — a genuine skill to be learned, not a temporary state to be endured and moved past. He directly separates grief from loss: grieving, in his own words, doesn't require loss to 'be detonated or appear' — it is a standing capacity, while bereavement specifically concerns the absence of something once present. A real, personal and unplanned disclosure surfaces partway through: Jenkinson reveals he received a real medical diagnosis roughly 16 months before this recording, involving what he describes as slow, observable neurological degeneration — offering a real, striking image of watching himself 'lose every third frame' like an old flip-book, while a part of him that isn't degenerating watches the rest degenerate. He distinguishes a vow (a present-moment commitment requiring no future) from a promise (inherently future-reliant), arguing hope itself functions as a promissory structure and asking directly what a 'vow equivalent' to hope might look like — his own real answer: being fully occupied by the necessities of the present moment, rather than banking on a future outcome. A real, specific literary reference closes the grief-and-joy discussion: Jenkinson cites 'Waiting in Line,' a real poem by William Stafford (paraphrased here rather than quoted at length, given real copyright limits), on the capacity to stand at a street corner and bid the whole of life goodbye. Jenkinson relays a real, secondhand story of a plaque placed near a rapidly receding Icelandic glacier reading, in his own paraphrase, 'we know what's happening, we know what must be done, only you will know whether we did it' — offered directly as a model for confronting large-scale crisis without false reassurance. Real, direct, unhedged advice closes the conversation: asked what he'd change with a magic wand, Jenkinson doesn't propose a policy or technology but a disposition — 'slow the fuck down,' arguing urgency, grievance, and despair can all be slowed without courting real danger, and that slowed momentum is 'a friend to genius and to wisdom.' Closes with real, direct advice for younger listeners specifically: 'it's not gonna get better,' and the necessary work ahead must be undertaken without the emotional payoff of feeling certain it's succeeding."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note, unusually severe for this graph: this transcript's own speaker attribution was extensively missing or incorrect throughout — MJSullivan's own words, 'one of the worst transcripts I've seen yet' — with only a handful of turns manually corrected by him near the end. The discussion summary above reflects a careful, good-faith reconstruction based on each speaker's distinctive register and the conversation's own question-and-answer logic, not a verified, cleanly-attributed source; residual uncertainty likely remains in a few genuinely ambiguous turns. UPDATED 2026-09-02 with the real, official show notes, which resolved a real, flagged uncertainty from the reconstruction: 'Ajit Ky' is confirmed as Ajit Varki, real UC San Diego researcher, author of 'Denial: Self-Deception, False Beliefs, and the Origins of the Human Mind.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ajit Varki and his book Denial (real, directly cited, a brief tangent rather than sustained engagement, not built); William Stafford (real, directly cited poet, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How Do You Become Who You Want to Be?: The Science Behind Identity, Purpose, and Motivation"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Nate hosts social cognitive neuroscientist Taylor Guthrie (University of Oregon PhD, Distinguished Teaching Award) for a real, technical deep dive into how the brain constructs a sense of self — grounded throughout in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex's real, replicated activation pattern whenever a person processes self-relevant information (traits describing oneself, ownership, closeness to others). Develops a real, direct distinction between extrinsic identity (built from external validation, performing a role for recognition) and intrinsic identity (built from genuine reflective alignment), applying this framework to addiction recovery via colleague Elliot Berkman's real 'identity-value model' — successful behavior change tends to come from adopting an identity ('I'm a healthy person') rather than pursuing a discrete goal ('lose 20 pounds'). Already cited by two other episodes in this graph (RR-26 and Frankly_143) as this graph's own primary source for the Default Mode Network — confirmed directly here, not inferred."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TaylorGuthrie ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/191-taylor-guthrie> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_lBKrPvDJo8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/TGS-191-Taylor-Guthrie-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, substantive philosophical disagreement structures the episode's most pointed section: Guthrie directly names his own real tension with Robert Sapolsky's hard determinism (Nate's own prior guest, already built in this graph), while still crediting Sapolsky's core claim — the present moment is genuinely, heavily constrained by one's full personal and evolutionary history. Guthrie's own resolution draws on Viktor Frankl's real 'space between stimulus and response' framing (which he notes Nate has himself invoked on other episodes) to argue this isn't the same as denying will entirely: 'it's not free will, but it's still will.' The real, concrete center of the conversation is attention itself — Guthrie's own direct claim that humans are consciously aware of roughly 2% of what the brain actually processes at any moment, used to explain the real, common experience of knowing you value nature while still reflexively reaching for a phone mid-hike, reframed not as hypocrisy but as real-time resource allocation under fluctuating internal states. The episode's most personally revealing moment comes in its final minutes: Guthrie discloses, largely unprompted, that becoming a father in the weeks before this recording directly reshaped his relationship to pessimistic futures content he'd previously consumed 'voraciously' — finding it newly difficult to sit with doom-oriented material while actively raising children he wants to see reach their full potential. His own real, concrete magic-wand answer is notably specific rather than abstract: eliminating short-form video content (TikTok, Instagram Reels) specifically, grounded in his own real classroom practice — a three-week 'go without' addiction assignment where giving up short-form video reliably proves harder for students than expected, revealing how powerfully these platforms have colonized attention. Closes on a real, direct piece of advice distinct from the episode's neuroscience framing: 'choose your groups wisely,' arguing that much of what feels like intrinsically-held identity is in fact shaped by which relationships and communities a person has embedded themselves in."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Elliot Berkman (Guthrie's own real colleague, cited directly for the identity-value model of addiction/behavior change); Dan McAdams (real narrative-identity researcher, cited directly for his 'redemptive arc vs. contamination' framework of trauma response); Slavoj Žižek (real philosopher, cited — though not by name in the excerpt reviewed — for his framing of ideology as defining 'what you're blind to being able to believe'). This episode is now confirmed, not just cross-referenced, as this graph's own primary real source on the Default Mode Network, matching what both RR-26 and Frankly_143 had already asserted about it before this build existed."@en ;
    .


