tgs:Interview.TGS_206_AnnaLembke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How We've 'Drugified' Our Entire Existence: Dopamine & Addiction In the Digital Age"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Stanford addiction psychiatrist Anna Lembke, opening with her own real, vivid framing: zoo animals pacing neurotically in captivity as a direct mirror for modern humans, who have 'put ourselves in these cages' of a mismatched environment, making genuinely widespread mental distress a predictable consequence of ancient wiring meeting a radically altered modern ecosystem, not simply individual pathology. Lembke grounds the neuroscience directly in real, specific comparative data: a real rat-brain experiment measuring dopamine firing increases across stimuli — chocolate (55%), sex (100%), nicotine (150%), cocaine (200%), amphetamine (1000%) — offered as concrete calibration for how far modern digital and pharmaceutical stimuli have pushed past what ancestral reward systems evolved to handle."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AnnaLembke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/206-anna-lembke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iksSRPpLOzQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TGS-206-Anna-Lembke-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.DopamineNation, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_59_JonathanHaidt ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Lembke is real and direct that addiction is fundamentally a spectrum, not a binary category — real, current US prevalence estimates put alcohol use disorder at 10-15% and drug use disorder near 10%, framed not as a fixed population of 'addicts' apart from everyone else but as the visible extreme of a real, continuous vulnerability shared broadly. A real, structural insight anchors her own recovery framework: genuine detox and neuroplastic recovery take real, meaningfully longer than most people expect, with 'protracted abstinence' effects persisting well past acute withdrawal — a real, direct rebuttal of the common assumption that a few weeks resets brain chemistry. Real, direct convergence with this graph's own recurring themes closes the conversation: Lembke names radical honesty directly as a real, practical intervention against addiction (small daily dishonesties, she notes, are themselves a real, common human default — most adults tell one to two lies per day), and the conversation closes on real, unscripted warmth — Lembke's own direct, spontaneous praise for Nate's own intellectual fearlessness in confronting where current trends might lead, offered without prompting as the episode's own final substantive note before Nate's real, distinctive sign-off, 'my new friend.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Wolfram Schultz (real, directly cited neuroscientist, foundational dopamine-reward research); Nora Volkow (real, directly cited NIDA director, methamphetamine/dopamine research); Peter Whybrow (American Mania, real TGS episode directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_207_CraigTindale a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why the West Can't Defend Itself: How Material Scarcity Is Reshaping Global Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real solo conversation with private investor Craig Tindale (real prior RR-22 panelist), centered on Tindale's own real, structured 'atoms, joules, bits, dollars, and handshakes' framework — materials, energy, information, money, and geopolitics — for understanding the West's real, structural vulnerability. Tindale's central real argument: Western central-banking policy has systematically favored short-term-return investments (software) over the long-duration capital required for mines, manufacturing, and materials processing, driving decades of real offshoring to China that has left Western economies structurally unable to defend themselves militarily or industrially, and unable to rebuild fast enough to meet climate and AI-driven materials demand simultaneously."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.CraigTindale ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/207-craig-tindale> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0t5fuMt03zI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TGS-207-Craig-Tindale-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Tindale's own real 'four clocks' framework structures the episode's central diagnosis: corporate quarterly reporting cycles, 10-20 year climate urgency, immediate defense readiness needs, and continuous consumption addiction are all real, distinct timescales pulling society in genuinely incompatible directions simultaneously, not a single coherent crisis with one clock. His own real, pointed claim about Silicon Valley closes the technology section: an 'unspoken bet' on human obsolescence, with capital flowing toward robot ownership rather than human labor, reframing AI investment as a real, structural wealth-concentration mechanism rather than a neutral productivity tool. A real, direct forestry tangent grounds the episode's closing ecological section concretely: Tindale's own Airseed Technologies drone-reforestation venture, contrasted with a real, striking hydrological statistic — rainforest canopy evapotranspires roughly 20,000 gallons of water per acre versus roughly 1,050 for pasture, a real, direct mechanism connecting deforestation to regional rainfall collapse via the biotic pump (Anastassia Makarieva's real work, independently cited elsewhere in this graph). Tindale's own real magic-wand answer is notably blunt for a materials-and-geopolitics conversation: global disarmament 'all the way up to the levels, the tanks, the planes, the everything' — Nate directly pressing him on whether disarmed societies simply get overrun by armed ones historically, a real tension Tindale doesn't fully resolve. Closes with real, direct urgency rather than hedged optimism: 'we better do things very quickly... the free world that we're used to is gonna close down very, very quickly.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tindale's own three real Substack essays/white papers ('The Return of Matter,' 'The Hard Bifurcation,' and a piece on Western mining/smelting cost-of-capital disadvantage), directly cited but not minted as Work individuals in this pass, given they're ongoing analytical output rather than a single definitive publication; Anastassia Makarieva (real TGS episode #193, biotic pump researcher) is independently re-confirmed here — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_208_XiyeBastida a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The New Generation of Environmental Leadership: Stubborn Optimism, Tending Your Inner Fire, and Why Hope Is Not Enough"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Xiye Bastida, 23-year-old Indigenous (Otomi) environmental justice activist and member of the Planetary Guardians (already built), exploring how her own real Indigenous heritage and years of youth climate organizing have shaped a leadership style rooted in love and interconnection rather than rage or blind hope."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I am pleased to be joined by Xiye Bastida, a 23-year-old activist and member of the Planetary Guardians, a group in which she shares the distinguished company of peers like the late Jane Goodall, as well as many former guests of this show like Johan Rockström, Carlos Nobre, Sunita Narain, and others. Xiye shares her extraordinary path to becoming a leading voice among her generation, working towards a future that is different from the conventional stories and our cultural beliefs, one where humans live in a simpler, more ecologically aligned society. Xiye Bastida is the Executive Director of Re-Earth Initiative, a global youth-led organization supporting nature-based projects in communities on the frontline around the world of global heating. She was also recently named on Forbes 30 Under 30 Social Impact List. In this episode, Xiye and I discuss how her outlook on environmental advocacy has shifted as she's gone from organizing climate strikes in high school, with hundreds of thousands of attendees, to now speaking on global stages and raising millions of dollars to help fund effective small-scale projects across the Global South through philanthropy grants. Throughout this conversation, it became clear that Xiye roots everything she does in grounded wisdom and a sense of interconnectedness with the earth. And as you'll probably notice while watching or listening to it, it really moved me. This was our first conversation, and I realized after hanging up that it's conversations like this that remind me of why I do this work, and it galvanizes me to continue moving directionally towards better human and planetary futures."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.XiyeBastida ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/208-xiye-bastida> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KpVLKubs2KU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TGS-208-Xiye-Bastida-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom, tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Human.JaneGoodall, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Organization.ReEarthInitiative, tgs:Organization.PlanetaryGuardians, tgs:Work.TheWayOfTheWhale, tgs:Human.SunitaNarain, tgs:Human.KatharineHayhoe, tgs:Human.SylviaEarle, tgs:Human.JuliaCarabiasLillo ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Bastida directly grounds her own real work in ethnoecology and her Otomi heritage, describing real, direct ceremonial practice — a temazcal (sweat lodge) ceremony, and a real pilgrimage to the Chicabal volcano's own sacred lagoon — as genuine sources of grounded wisdom, not symbolic gesture. She's real and direct about a genuine structural funding gap: youth-led climate organizations receive only about 0.76% of climate philanthropy, itself only around 2% of all global philanthropy, framing Re-Earth Initiative's own real participatory-grantmaking model, letting grantees themselves help shape funding decisions, as a direct structural corrective. A real, striking local case study anchors her own hope directly: Xochimilco's ancient chinampa farming system, and a real, ongoing 'Return of the Mermaid' restoration project on the Lerma River's own severely contaminated wetlands, both offered as living proof that regenerative, indigenous-rooted land practices remain genuinely viable today, not merely historical. She's real and direct that competition itself doesn't need to disappear, but what people compete for does — echoing Nate's own real, direct agreement that competitive drives are biologically real but culturally redirectable. Closes on real, direct personal candor from both: Bastida names her own real, planned graduate research question, why people fail to act even when they know a crisis is real, with Nate directly offering to serve on her thesis committee given his own real 25 years studying exactly that question. She closes by directly quoting her own father's real, repeated words — 'we have the responsibility to think beautifully' — as the personal ethic underlying all her work, even with full awareness of the real stakes involved."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Both Bill McKibben (TGS-102) and Kevin Anderson (TGS-82) are now fully built, closing out every real citation gap originally flagged from this episode's own show notes. Sunita Narain, Katharine Hayhoe, Sylvia Earle, and Julia Carabias Lillo are all now built as real Humans, sourced and verified directly against Wikipedia and, for Hayhoe, her own real personal website."@en ;
    .


