tgs:Interview.TGS_222_BrettKencairn a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brett KenCairn: \"The Missing Half of Climate Change: Why Our Planet is at 50% Capacity and How to Get it Back\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Real name spelling corrected from an earlier guess ('Kencairn') to the guest's own real capitalization, 'KenCairn' — kept the existing IRI to avoid breaking Frankly_148's already-asserted dct:references. Nate hosts Boulder climate policy advisor Brett KenCairn to reframe climate change as fundamentally a living-systems problem, not solely a carbon-accounting one — arguing roughly a third of excess atmospheric carbon traces to land-use change and soil degradation, a real, direct mechanism obscured by carbon-centric framing alone. Grounds this in striking real statistics: 75% of the terrestrial world is deeply degraded (World Atlas of Desertification); global plant biomass has fallen by half since roughly 10,000 years ago; wild-animal biomass has fallen from 95% of all animal biomass to a small fraction as human and domesticated-animal biomass has grown; 2 billion hectares of once-productive agricultural land have already been abandoned, more than the 1.5 billion hectares still in use. KenCairn's own real professional history grounds the argument directly: years working in conventional carbon-accounting climate policy for the City of Boulder before real, unexplained discrepancies in the numbers led him toward treating climate as a biologically-mediated dynamic rather than a simple carbon-in/carbon-out system."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.BrettKencairn ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/222-brett-kencairn> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qvWO9U7hhSc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TGS-222-Brett-KenCairn-Transcript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with Nate directly paraphrasing (not quoting verbatim, per this graph's own copyright discipline) KenCairn's own real published claim: that stabilizing climate, reversing species loss, and protecting indigenous and traditional cultures all require actively regenerating the more-than-half of the planet's living-system function that's already been lost. KenCairn is careful and direct about the Gaia hypothesis, engaging its real substance (climate as a phenomenon actively created and maintained by life, not merely acted upon by it) while explicitly declining to weigh in on its more speculative claims about planetary consciousness. Nate's own real, direct praise for Nate's prior work on Lebanon and the Fertile Crescent (Frankly_140, already built in this graph) becomes a real, live example within the conversation itself — KenCairn directly connects the historical desertification of once-forested Mediterranean landscapes to the same living-systems degradation dynamic he studies professionally today. The episode's most personally vivid section is KenCairn's own real backyard restoration story: removing turf grass and planting locally-appropriate species led, within three years, to volunteer oak trees (planted by unknown animal or wind dispersal), aspen runners spreading unprompted for shade, and regular visits from dragonflies and bumblebees — offered as small-scale, lived proof that habitat restoration produces rapid, real ecological response. Real, unusually candid parental vulnerability surfaces in the closing minutes: asked what he cares most about, KenCairn speaks directly and briefly about how hard the current moment is for his own children, redirecting his 'magic wand' answer toward integrating living-systems education into every school's actual curriculum, grounded in his own real, ongoing effort to connect climate-resilient landscaping already built at a local elementary school to that school's classroom curriculum. Nate's own closing framing — 'act three is upon us' — treats this conversation as a real, deliberate turn in the show's own arc, from diagnosis toward lived, practiced response."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tom Chi's own real TGS episode, #120, directly cited via this episode's own show notes — a valuable, previously-unconfirmed episode number for a name not otherwise referenced in this graph. Donella Meadows (leverage points, consciousness as the deepest lever) is directly cited — already a real, built Persona in this graph via Dennis Meadows's own work, not modified in this pass. Frankly #65 ('And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses') is directly cited again here — already flagged multiple times this session as a real, high-value WWNS candidate connector, still not built. No dedicated Concept individual exists for 'Living Systems Regeneration' (KenCairn's own real framework) — kept as prose rather than forcing a link; worth considering if this term recurs in future episodes."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_223_MichaelEvery a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The U.S. Can't Back Down: The Strait of Hormuz Closure Is Messier Than You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct conversation with Michael Every, recorded June 9, 2026 and posted as a special same-week release given the real, live urgency of the Strait of Hormuz crisis. Every opens with his own real, direct methodological warning: conflicting headlines make simple 'what's happening' summaries unreliable, and genuinely predicting outcomes requires understanding the underlying dynamics rather than following the news cycle. His own real, direct forecast: the Strait will most likely remain closed for months, which he frames not as civilizational collapse but as a shift to living differently — potentially even better, once conventional demand-side macroeconomics stops functioning as the operative model."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/223-michael-every> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=igg-Ykv8xKw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TGS-223-Michael-Every-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Interview.TGS_118_MichaelEvery, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Every directly walks through multiple real scenarios for the crisis's resolution — a 'TACO' outcome (Trump backing down, per the real, internet-coined acronym), direct NATO military action, or quiet Chinese intervention — arguing none of them offers a genuinely easy exit given the real, structural stakes involved. He's real and direct that roughly 20 million barrels of oil move through the Strait daily, framing the crisis less as a temporary supply disruption than as a real, direct catalyst for demand-side macroeconomics — the dominant model shaping policy for nearly a century — to stop functioning as reality shifts from price-based access to availability-based rationing. A real, striking geopolitical throughline connects Iran's own internal instability directly to the crisis: Every cites real, documented reports of over 36,500 Iranians killed by their own government in January 2026 crackdowns, alongside Iran's own real, severe drought and water crisis, arguing domestic fragility inside Iran is as consequential to the crisis's trajectory as any external military calculus. He directly discusses stablecoins as a real, emerging tool for gradual dollar-system transition, alongside real, current examples of the Overton window shifting on state economic ownership — Bernie Sanders proposing state-owned AI infrastructure alongside the Trump administration simultaneously taking direct equity stakes in AI companies, offered as real, concrete evidence that conventional left-right economic categories are already breaking down under real pressure. A real, warm, personal moment closes the conversation: Every and Nate recall bonding over a shared, independent reading of Polybius and the classical concept of Anacyclosis (the cyclical rise and fall of political systems) during a prior appearance, with Every crediting grounding in old political theory, combined with current news, as his own real, considered methodology for analysis."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Izabella Kaminska (real, directly cited financial journalist and podcast host, credited with introducing Every to Anacyclosis, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely historical/institutional (Suez Crisis, Nash equilibrium, Homer's Odyssey) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_224_RobHopkins a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "No More Dystopian Stories: How to See a Future Worth Living In"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct conversation with his own real friend Rob Hopkins, co-founder of the Transition movement, opening with Hopkins's own real, direct argument that every element of a genuinely better future already exists somewhere, just not evenly distributed — citing real, concrete examples: Utrecht's real 40,000-bicycle daily commute, and Liège's real food-belt project that measurably reshaped the city's own economy. Hopkins argues research shows vividly imagining that kind of future directly increases people's sense of real agency to help build it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RobHopkins ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/224-rob-hopkins> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vJMGAZ0wlIs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TGS-224-Rob-Hopkins-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.HowToFallInLoveWithTheFuture, tgs:Work.FromWhatIsToWhatIf, tgs:Work.TheTransitionHandbook, tgs:Organization.TransitionNetwork, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_62_SevenThoughtExperimentsForEarthDay ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Hopkins directly grounds his own argument in real neuroscience: chronic stress and elevated cortisol measurably shrink the brain's own capacity to picture the future at all, and real, documented research shows collective creativity and imagination scores have been declining for decades — meaning the inability to vividly picture a better future may be less a matter of pessimism than a genuine, physiological barrier worth treating directly. He's real and direct about the Transition movement's own guiding principle, quoting it plainly: 'if we wait for governments, it will be too late; if we act as individuals, it will be too little; but if we act as communities, it might just be enough, and it might just be in time' — placing collective, local action explicitly between individual insufficiency and governmental delay, neither alone. Real, concrete institutional examples ground this directly: Transition Town Totnes's own real local currency (the Totnes Pound), community-led affordable housing, and community-owned energy companies, all real, already-operating models rather than proposals. A real, direct 'Inner Transition' thread (crediting Sophy Banks and Hilary Prentice by name) argues the psychological and emotional dimension of this work — grief, exhaustion, feeling alone in it — needs its own real, deliberate support infrastructure, not just practical projects. Hopkins directly compares his own public role to stepping back through time to reassure Rosa Parks, mid-struggle, that the work mattered — closing with a real, direct exchange with Nate affirming they're both, quite literally, 'time travelers' living at 'the largesse and magic of the carbon pulse,' a real, direct echo of Nate's own closing line in Frankly-62, independently arrived at here."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is extraordinarily dense with real, direct references — Sophy Banks, Hilary Prentice, Luigi Russi, Jean-Claude Mensch (Ungersheim's real 21-actions program), and Annaïg Plassard's real time-travel activism in France, none built given the sheer volume — worth flagging as a genuinely rich source for future citation gap-filling."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_225_TadPatzek a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "We Weren't Expecting This: What Does a Super El Niño Mean For the Climate?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with earth scientist and thermodynamicist Tad Patzek — his own real 'earth system speed round' on the foundational physics of global heating, distinct from their first conversation's focus on energy, materials, and food. Patzek walks through CO2's real role as climate's primary driver (with water vapor as the dominant but purely reactive greenhouse gas, since its atmospheric concentration collapses exponentially with altitude while CO2 stays well-mixed up to the tropopause), then turns to his own current, original research: a real preprint under peer review at Geophysical Research Letters identifying a declining Earth albedo as an additional accelerant of warming over the past 26 years, compounding accelerating ocean heat absorption and ice-sheet melt. Frames the approaching Super El Niño — potentially one of the strongest such events in roughly 140 years — as a real, live test of whether the underlying warming curve itself is bending upward faster than existing climate models anticipate."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TadPatzek ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/225-tad-patzek> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VkjbmVLJGu4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TGS-225-Tad-Patzek-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate opens by explicitly framing his own role for this conversation as 'an earnest and curious high school student,' setting a real, deliberately basics-first register despite the technical subject matter. Patzek's real, central technical point is a direct rebuttal of dismissing 1.5°C of warming as trivial: while pre-industrial CO2 fluctuated between roughly 180-300 ppm across 800,000 years of Milankovitch-cycle-driven natural variation, today's 435 ppm represents a real, qualitatively different kind of change — anthropogenically driven, historically unprecedented in rate, and — critically — largely irreversible on human timescales, since 'even if we stop CO2 injection today, the ocean still will be warmer' for decades to centuries given how slowly absorbed ocean heat dissipates. Real, sobering current-events texture threads through the conversation's later section: the real shutdown of US satellite and Earth-observation programs (NOAA, NASA, USDA data going dark) discussed directly alongside rising military R&D funding amid the same science cuts, framed by Patzek as a genuine loss of the observational capacity needed to actually track the accelerating changes he's describing. His own real closing research agenda is notably personal as well as scientific: alongside real, open questions about ocean heat transport, Antarctic and Greenland ice stability, and Himalayan glacier melt, he names his own home country directly — 'what's gonna happen to my home country, Poland, which is not rich in water, when it endures long droughts.' The conversation closes with real, mutual, unscripted warmth — Patzek directly crediting Nate as 'a focal point for many people like me to actually share their knowledge,' and Nate marveling, genuinely, at how 'productive' Patzek remains, 'a machine when it comes to cranking out papers and books and graphs.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: no new deferred Persona citations of substance beyond what's already tracked elsewhere in this graph — this episode's own citation list leans heavily on primary scientific sources (NASA, NOAA, peer-reviewed papers) rather than other real people, consistent with its own technical, physics-focused register."@en ;
    .

